    "evidence_text": "the KL divergence between the language model of INLINEFORM5 and articles in INLINEFORM6 .\n\nWe combine this measure with the entity overlap of INLINEFORM7 and INLINEFORM8 .\n\nLow scores indicate low novelty for the entity profile INLINEFORM10 .\n\nDKL((n') || (n)).\n\nHere we introduce the evaluation setup and analyze the results for the article\u2013entity (AEP) placement task.\n\nWe only report the evaluation metrics for the `relevant' news-entity pairs.\n\nBaselines.\n\nWe consider the following baselines for this task.\n\nB1.\n\nThe first baseline uses only the salience-based\n\nfeatures by Dunietz and Gillick  .\n\nLearning Models.\n\nWe use Random Forests (RF)  .\n\nWe learn the RF on all computed features in Table TABREF21 .\n\nThe higher the margin score the more robust the model.\n\nMetrics.\n\nPerformance.\n\nFigure FIGREF55 shows the results for the years 2009 and\n\nThe results show the precision\u2013recall curve.\n\nIn addition, at each confidence score we can compute the corresponding recall for the `relevant' label.\n\nWe measure the significance through the t-test statistic and get a p-value of\n\nINLINEFORM1 .\n\nThe improvement for recall is INLINEFORM3 R=+0.4.\n\nRobustness.\n\nThe results are particularly promising considering the fact that the distribution between our two classes is highly skewed.\n\nOn average the number of `relevant' pairs account for only around INLINEFORM0 of all pairs.\n\nThe authority and novelty features account to a further improvement in\n\npre-computed templates.\n\nWith the\n\nThe performance on average is around P=0.66 across all classes.\n\nprecision of P=0.17, with a uniform distribution across the years.\n\nHere we show the performance of INLINEFORM0 decomposed for the different entity classes.\n\nSpecifically we analyze the 27 classes in Figure FIGREF42 .\n\nThe results are already stable for the year range INLINEFORM2 .\n\nThese additionally represent classes with a sufficient number of training instances for the years\n\nThe lowest F1 score is for the Criminal and Television classes.\n\nHowever, this is directly correlated with the insufficient number of instances.\n\nThe baseline approaches for the ASP task perform poorly.\n\nS1, based on lexical similarity, has a varying performance for different entity classes.\n\nThe best performance is achieved for the class Person \u2013 Politics, with P=0.43.\n\nFor different entity classes different features and combination of features is necessary.\n\nThe score we achieve shows that our model predicts with high confidence with INLINEFORM3 .\n\nThe last analysis is the impact we have on expanding entity profiles\n\nIn the future, we will enhance our work by extracting facts from the suggested news articles.\n\nResults suggest that the news content cited in entity pages comes from the first paragraphs.\n\nHowever, challenging task such as the canonicalization and chronological ordering of facts, still remain.",
